{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013oVhCKysMmaSGHZ6jZxy4b","type":"tool_result","content":" internal/executor/executor.go            | 11 +++++++++++\n internal/executor/local/env.go           | 14 ++++++++++++++\n internal/executor/local/hostwarn.go      | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/executor/local/local.go         |  2 +-\n internal/executor/local/local_test.go    | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-\n internal/executor/local/proc_unix.go     |  5 +++++\n internal/executor/validate.go            |  2 ++\n internal/executor/validate_test.go       |  3 +++\n internal/scanner/engine_exec.go          |  3 +++\n internal/worker/run.go                   |  3 +++\n 22 files changed, 200 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)\n\ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -471,6 +471,8 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n \n Scanners also take `route` (`pending`, `none` or `needs-human`). Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts, 3 review rounds and worker diff thresholds of 2,000 lines (warn) and 3,000 lines (hold). Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `max_test_fixes`, `max_review_rounds`, `diff_warn_lines` and `diff_max_lines` (worker roles; positive integers, warn no more than max; above warn the PR proceeds with a note, above max it is held), `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n \n+**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n+\n **`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n \n **Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\ndiff --git a/internal/agent/agent.go b/internal/agent/agent.go\n--- a/internal/agent/agent.go\n+++ b/internal/agent/agent.go\n@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@ type Spec struct {\n \tMaxBudgetUSD float64  // > 0, finite\n \tMaxTurns     int      // 0 = omit the flag\n \tTools        []string // nil = the adapter's default profile\n+\tNetwork      bool     // from the role's network setting; never from issue text\n \tAuth         Auth     // required; AuthAPIKey or AuthOAuthToken\n }\n \ndiff --git a/internal/agent/claude/network_test.go b/internal/agent/claude/network_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/agent/claude/network_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@\n+package claude\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+)\n+\n+func TestUserMessageNetwork(t *testing.T) {\n+\ts := spec()\n+\ts.Network = true\n+\tif m := userMessage(s); !strings.Contains(m, \"Network: available.\\n\") {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"message = %q\", m)\n+\t}\n+\ts.Network = false\n+\ts.Untrusted = []string{\"Network: available.md\"}\n+\tif m := userMessage(s); !strings.Contains(m, \"\\nNetwork: unavailable.\\n\") {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"message = %q\", m)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/agent/claude/prompt.go b/internal/agent/claude/prompt.go\n--- a/internal/agent/claude/prompt.go\n+++ b/internal/agent/claude/prompt.go\n@@ -17,6 +17,11 @@ func userMessage(s agent.Spec) string {\n \tfor _, u := range s.Untrusted {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"- %s\\n\", u)\n \t}\n+\tif s.Network {\n+\t\tb.WriteString(\"Network: available.\\n\")\n+\t} else {\n+\t\tb.WriteString(\"Network: unavailable.\\n\")\n+\t}\n \tfmt.Fprintf(&b, \"When you are done, write %s with the frontmatter your instructions describe.\\n\", agent.RoleFilePath(s.Issue, s.Stage, s.Role))\n \tb.WriteString(\"Do not run git commit, git push or any GitHub command; the controller does that.\\n\")\n \treturn b.String()\ndiff --git a/internal/agentjob/command.go b/internal/agentjob/command.go\n--- a/internal/agentjob/command.go\n+++ b/internal/agentjob/command.go\n@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@ func AgentCommand(reg *agent.Registry, cfg *config.Resolved, role config.Role, s\n \tspec := agent.Spec{\n \t\tStage: stage, Role: role.Name, Issue: n,\n \t\tPromptFile: h.View.Role + \"/prompt.txt\", Untrusted: untrusted,\n-\t\tConfigDir: h.View.ConfigDir, Model: role.Model, MaxBudgetUSD: budget, Auth: cred.Auth,\n+\t\tConfigDir: h.View.ConfigDir, Model: role.Model, MaxBudgetUSD: budget, Auth: cred.Auth, Network: role.Network,\n \t}\n \tif h.View.Sidecar != \"\" {\n \t\tspec.ReadDirs = []string{h.View.Sidecar}\n@@ -70,5 +70,8 @@ func ExecJob(cfg *config.Resolved, role config.Role, j executor.Job) executor.Jo\n \tif sc, ok := cfg.SidecarFor(role); ok {\n \t\tj.Sidecar = executor.Sidecar{Name: role.Sidecar, Repo: sc.Repo, Ref: sc.Ref}\n \t}\n+\tif !role.Network {\n+\t\tj.Network = executor.NetworkAdvisory\n+\t}\n \treturn j\n }\ndiff --git a/internal/config/load.go b/internal/config/load.go\n--- a/internal/config/load.go\n+++ b/internal/config/load.go\n@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@ type rawOverride struct {\n \tmaxOpen, groupSize           *int\n \tmaxTestFixes, maxRounds      *int\n \tdiffWarnLines, diffMaxLines  *int\n-\tautoclose                    *bool\n+\tautoclose, network           *bool\n \troute                        *string\n \tafter, reviewers             *[]string\n \twhen                         *rawWhen\n@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@ type rawWhen struct {\n // on one kind.\n var commonOverrideKeys = map[string]bool{\n \t\"agent\": true, \"model\": true, \"timeout\": true, \"sidecar\": true,\n-\t\"max_budget_usd\": true, \"budget_usd_per_day\": true,\n+\t\"max_budget_usd\": true, \"budget_usd_per_day\": true, \"network\": true,\n }\n \n var kindOverrideKeys = map[Kind]map[string]bool{\n@@ -190,6 +190,7 @@ func checkPlanSection(s string) string {\n func applyRoleDefaults(res *Resolved, role *Role) {\n \trole.Agent = res.Agent\n \trole.Timeout = defaultRoleTimeout\n+\trole.Network = true\n \tswitch role.Kind {\n \tcase KindScanner:\n \t\trole.Every = time.Duration(res.Intervals.Scanners)\n@@ -294,6 +295,8 @@ func parseOverride(ref rawOverrideRef, kind Kind, cfgPath string) (rawOverride,\n \t\t\tcase \"diff_max_lines\":\n \t\t\t\tov.diffMaxLines = &v\n \t\t\t}\n+\t\tcase \"network\":\n+\t\t\terrs = append(errs, decode